Lesson written and validated.
**Summary of what landed:**
- `courses/optyka-vs-sar-porownawcza-wizja-komputerowa/lessons/optyka-vs-sar-dwa-rozne-swiaty.json` — 5 sections: `theory` → `quiz` → `theory` → `code` → `quiz`, validates against `LessonSchema.safeParse`.
- Theory 1 (292 words, *Pasywna kamera, aktywny radar*) embeds the Hamburg side-by-side hero and explains the passive/active sensor distinction. Theory 2 (345 words, *Cztery różnice…*) embeds the speckle close-up and covers the four CV-breaking consequences (multiplicative noise, complex pixels, polarization, slant geometry) with minimal KaTeX per the user's "math only where it earns its keep" preference.
- Quiz 1 tests passive-vs-active understanding with three plausible distractors. Quiz 2 is multi-select about when SAR adds value (night/clouds/InSAR vs cases where color is what you actually need).
- Code section: `simulate_speckle(image, enl, seed)` with 4 tests (one visible smoke test, three hidden — shape, mean preservation, multiplicative variance scaling, ENL dependency) plus full reference solution. Uses only `numpy` so no `requiresPackages` needed.
- 4 lesson-level sources (Moreira 2013 tutorial, NASA Earthdata, Wikipedia SAR, Sentinel-2 ESA guide) plus section-level sources on both theory beats.
**Imagery (all in `assets/images/`):**
- `hamburg-optical-vs-sar-side-by-side.png` — composite I generated from real Sentinel-1 + Sentinel-2 quicklooks fetched from CDSE for 2024-09-28 over Hamburg (S2 cloud-covered, S1 clear — exact pedagogical pairing).
- `sentinel1-speckle-closeup.png` — 160×160 grayscale crop of the same Sentinel-1 scene showing speckle texture.
- Source quicklooks (`sentinel1-hamburg-quicklook.png`, `sentinel2-hamburg-quicklook.jpg`, `sentinel1-rotterdam-port-quicklook.png`) left in place for downstream lessons.
Note: the SKILL.md describes a standalone `image` widget (US-050), but it isn't in the current registry / schema — only `plotImage` is, and that requires quantitative axes. I therefore embedded the side-by-side hero as an inline markdown image inside theory 1 rather than as a separate widget. The lesson still meets the 1+ theory / 2–5 widget shape (5 total sections, ends on a quiz, no two theory back-to-back).
